The auditor rejected stage 4 at `7a7731d` on one finding, and I've sent it word for word to the builder as round 1 of at most 4. The verifier's verdict on the same revision hasn't arrived yet. If it raises anything new, I'll forward that to the builder separately.

**The finding:** changing the time of a recurring series fails when one of its occurrences is already past its cancellation cutoff, even if that occurrence wouldn't actually change. The spec says an unchanged occurrence is a no-op, and only real changes check their cutoff. Two cases fail with a 409 instead of succeeding:
- every occurrence is already at the requested time;
- one occurrence is unchanged and another is really changed.

The auditor added a regression test for this, S4-049 in commit `10d0a2c`. It takes 1–2 minutes because it waits for a booking to cross its cutoff.

**Everything else the auditor checked passed:**
- The API suite passed 217/217 and the browser suite 39/39 on its own image, and the supplied checks report `claimed stage: 4`.
- In the code:
  - the replan search covers every option, and its tie-breaks are correct;
  - each write bumps the restaurant's revision exactly once;
  - applying a plan is all-or-nothing.
- The new public `/closures` endpoint is acceptable. It exposes no diner data and leaves existing responses unchanged.

**What the builder must do:**
- work out each occurrence's new time first, and run the cutoff and policy checks only on occurrences that really change;
- commit a new revision on top of the auditor's test;
- pass S4-049, rerun all suites and the supplied check, and report back.

